## Findings recorded for the orchestrator (no spec rows amended)

1. **Oracle observation on c9/p9, committed-evidence-consistent but worth knowing:** the oracle's anon/`_edge_style` rules are quirkier than the spec prose. Verified under the pinned oracle: directed edges follow the last unit ✓, but arrow-less styles come from `_edge_style`'s whole-pattern homogeneity regexes — including an **unescaped `(..-)+`** that matches *any two chars ending in `-`*, so `- -[ b ]` is dot-dot-dash and `== [ b ]` (trailing space absorbed as a `= ` unit) is solid while `==[ b ]` is double. My p9 pins these; the spec's "style follows the last unit token" reads true for directed patterns only.
2. **Anon-node `#N` is the global object-id counter upstream** (saw `#0`, `#1`, `#3`, `#4` across multi-anon inputs — not always odd). The spec's odd-counter simplification matches all committed probe evidence; implemented as a dedicated odd counter. A left-edge anon (`[ ] --> [ a ]`) can yield `#0` upstream.
3. **Attr-block targets:** a standalone `{ … }` targets the oracle's stack-top = last **node** or closed **group**, never an edge (upstream errors on node-invalid keys like `style`; my parser stores verbatim per the ge.graph_model contract); `{ … }` before any object is silently dropped upstream — replicated.
4. **Deliberately unsupported (typed errors, follow-up candidates):** comma-separated node lists (`[ a ], [ b ] => [ c ]`), `|` autosplit, group-endpoint edges (`( G: [ a ] ) --> [ b ]` creates a group→node edge upstream), subclass class sections (`node.city { … }`), and the upstream `#+`→bold operator (unreachable post-comment-pass). All are outside c1–c9; none affect the txt_render round-trip.
5. Minor: pretender logs a cosmetic "Parse errors detected in edge.rs" warning (its Rust parser choking on char-literal unions) while reporting all green.
